.TH A 7 "2026" "a 0.1.0" "A language"
.SH NAME
a \- the A programming language
.SH DESCRIPTION
A is a small, C-like systems programming language. It compiles to native code
with no garbage collector and no hidden runtime, using the syntax of C where
possible. Programs are compiled by the
.BR ac (1)
compiler, which is itself written in x86-64 assembly and needs no external
assembler, linker or C library.
.PP
This page is a short overview. The complete reference is
.I /usr/share/doc/a-lang-common/reference/LANGUAGE.md
and the book "The A Programming Language"
(\fI/usr/share/doc/a-lang-common/html/\fR and the EPUB next to it).
.SH SYNTAX AT A GLANCE
.nf
int main(int argc, char** argv) {
    println("hello, world");
    return 0;
}
.fi
.PP
Top level declarations (functions, structs, classes, enums, constants, modules) may appear in
any order; a function can be called before the line that defines it. Statements end with
\fB;\fR, comments are \fB// line\fR and \fB/* block */\fR (they do not nest).
.SH TYPES
.TP
.B int\fR, \fBlong
signed 64-bit integer (aliases of each other)
.TP
.B char\fR, \fBbyte
unsigned 8-bit, used for text and raw bytes
.TP
.B bool
\fBtrue\fR / \fBfalse\fR; any value stored becomes 0 or 1
.TP
.B void\fR, \fBvoid*
no value; generic pointer
.TP
.BI T *
pointer to \fIT\fR
.TP
.BI T " name" [N]
fixed array of \fIN\fR elements
.TP
.BI struct " S" \fR,\ \fBclass " C"
aggregate types, with public/private members on classes (see
.B STRUCTS, CLASSES AND ENUMS
below)
.TP
.BI enum " E"
behaves like \fBint\fR; \fBenum E : uint8 { ... }\fR picks the storage type
.TP
.BR int8 ", " int16 ", " int32 ", " int64 ", " uint8 ", " uint16 ", " uint32 ", " uint64
integers of exactly that width
.TP
.BR size_t ", " uintptr_t " (" uint64 "), " ssize_t ", " intptr_t ", " ptrdiff_t " (" int64 )
C-compatible aliases
.PP
\fBsizeof(Type)\fR gives the size in bytes. Casts use C syntax: \fB(char)x\fR, \fB(int)p\fR.
Every variable (locals, arrays, structs, heap memory) is zero-initialised.
.SH LITERALS
Integers (\fB42\fR, \fB1_000_000\fR, \fB0xFF\fR, \fB0b1010\fR, with C suffixes accepted and
ignored), characters (\fB'a'\fR, \fB'\en'\fR, \fB'\ex41'\fR), strings (\fB"hello\en"\fR, NUL
terminated), \fBtrue\fR/\fBfalse\fR, \fBnull\fR.
.SH VARIABLES AND CONSTANTS
.nf
int a;                    // zero-initialised
int b = 5, c = b * 2;     // several declarators
var d = compute();        // type comes from the initialiser
const MAX = 100;          // compile-time constant
const int MASK = 0xF0;    // optionally typed
static_assert(sizeof(int) == 8);
.fi
A variable lives until the end of its block; an inner declaration may shadow an outer one.
Globals are initialised with a constant expression and live in \fBbss\fR when zero.
.SH OPERATORS AND STATEMENTS
The usual C arithmetic, bitwise, comparison, logical and assignment operators (including
compound assignment and \fB++\fR / \fB\-\-\fR), plus \fB?:\fR. Statements:
\fBif\fR/\fBelse\fR, \fBwhile\fR, \fBdo\fR...\fBwhile\fR, \fBfor\fR (including \fBfor (x in
collection)\fR), \fBswitch\fR (also over strings and enums), \fBbreak\fR, \fBcontinue\fR,
\fBreturn\fR, blocks.
.SH FUNCTIONS
Parameters and results are passed by value, including structs and classes (the callee gets a
copy; results pass through a hidden destination). Parameters may have default values and a
trailing \fB...\fR (read with \fBva_arg(i)\fR / \fBva_count()\fR). \fBprivate\fR restricts a
function to the file that defines it. Lambdas are
.BR "func(" params ")" " { ... }"
closures assignable to a \fBfunc\fR-typed variable.
.SH STRUCTS, CLASSES AND ENUMS
.B struct
is plain data; a
.B class
adds
.B public:
/
.B private:
sections, constructors, methods and \fBnew\fR. \fBenum E { A, B, C }\fR members are
\fBE.A\fR etc.; \fBE.count\fR is the number of members. Tuples \fB(int, char*)\fR group values
without declaring a struct.
.SH MODULES AND IMPORTS
.nf
import text;               // a module from the standard library or lib/
import "./helpers.a";      // a file relative to the importing source
.fi
\fBimport\fR looks for a real file next to the importing source first, then in the
compiler's embedded standard library, then in \fBlib/\fR, \fI/usr/lib/a/\fR and
\fI/usr/local/lib/a/\fR. \fBpublic\fR marks a top level declaration visible to importers (the
default is private to the module). \fIlib/std.a\fR is special: it is compiled into every
program automatically, with no \fBimport\fR needed.
.SH MEMORY
No garbage collector: \fBalloc(n)\fR / \fBfree(p)\fR manage the heap explicitly; the stack
(recursion, locals) is 8 MiB. Arrays and strings do not carry bounds at runtime \(em the
standard library's \fBcollections\fR module provides growable, bounds-checked containers.
.SH STANDARD LIBRARY
Over 100 modules written in A: text and formatting, collections, numbers and big integers,
data formats (JSON, CSV, base64, ...), cryptography and TLS 1.3, networking (sockets, HTTP,
mail, FTP), the C library compatibility layer (\fBimport clib;\fR), a GUI toolkit with a
display server, a terminal UI toolkit, and the editor libraries of AIDE. The full list with
signatures is in
.I LANGUAGE.md
section 12 and the generated API reference in the book.
.SH TARGETS
.B ac
is a cross compiler: the same source compiles, unchanged, for Linux (x86-64, ARM64, RISC-V 64,
LoongArch64), Windows (x86-64) and Android (ARM64, x86-64, RISC-V 64). See
.BR ac (1)
and
.I /usr/share/doc/a-lang-common/reference/TARGETS.md
for the \fB\-t\fR values and per-target notes.
.SH DIFFERENCES FROM C
No preprocessor macros with arguments (only object-like \fB#define\fR via \fB\-D\fR), no
\fBunion\fR, no variadic macros, no implicit int-to-pointer conversions, every declaration is
zero-initialised, and structs/classes are first-class value types passed and returned by value.
See \fILANGUAGE.md\fR section 15 for the complete list.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /usr/share/doc/a-lang-common/reference/*.md
The full language, standard library, networking, GUI, TUI and internals references.
.TP
.I /usr/share/doc/a-lang-common/examples/
Example programs.
.TP
.I /usr/share/doc/a-lang-common/html/\fR, \fI*.epub
